# Code cleanup

Apply **only** when the user asks to clean up, simplify, refactor for clarity, or extract/reuse helpers. Do not run a cleanup pass during normal feature or bugfix work.

## Scope (required)

Do **not** clean the whole repo. Limit work to:

1. **Uncommitted changes** — every file in `git status` (staged + unstaged + untracked that belong to the work), or
2. If the working tree is clean — files touched by **`HEAD` only** (`git show --name-only --pretty="" HEAD`)

Stay inside that set unless the user names extra paths. Prefer a few high-value edits over a broad rewrite.

## Goals

- Simplify structure **without changing logic or behavior** (behavior preservation beats purity)
- Extract repeated logic; add **intention** comments (why / contract), not narrations of the next line
- Match existing style (Python in RDCC; Arduino/C++ in `Interactive Object/`)

## Principles

- **KISS** — smallest clarifying change; no clever metaclass/macro tricks
- **DRY** — extract when the same *intent* appears 2+ times, not merely similar-looking code
- **YAGNI** — no new layers, protocols, or folders “for later”
- **SRP / SLAP** — one job per function; one abstraction level (orchestration vs detail)
- **SoC** — keep boundaries: `core/` owns data/playback/streaming, `ui/` displays and binds, `settings.py` holds defaults and wire contracts; firmware `settings.h` mirrors device-side knobs
- **Command–query** — readers don’t mutate playback/stream state; draw/plot paths don’t start I/O
- **Explicit over implicit** — named constants, guard clauses, clear Qt signal ownership

### Extract when / don’t

- **Do** if it removes real duplication or makes the parent readable at a glance
- **Don’t** for one-off UI wiring, or if data flow (picker → model → playback → OSC/serial) gets harder to follow

## Comments

Prefer a better name over a comment. When commenting, explain **intention, constraints, and consequences** — never restate the code.

### Modules / public APIs (Python)

- Document public classes/functions with docstrings: **what** the API does, plus a short **why / when** if non-obvious
- Call out contracts: units, pin order, inactive-pin sentinel, rates (Hz), ownership, side effects
- Skip private helpers unless the signature alone is misleading

```python
# ❌ BAD — narrates the signature
"""Sends OSC values to the object."""

# ✅ GOOD — role + intention
"""Send one frame of Pin_A…F floats plus timestamp string over OSC.
Inactive slots use WIRE_INACTIVE_PIN_SENTINEL so firmware ignores them.
"""
```

### Implementation (`.py` / firmware `.cpp` / `.ino`)

- Comment **non-obvious why**: Qt quirks, serial port exclusivity, OSC typetags, smoothing/floor math, idle mute
- Comment **invariants / assumptions** the next maintainer must not break (e.g. pin index 0 = Pin_A)
- Cross-link policy (`settings.py` / `settings.h`) instead of duplicating long tables
- Do **not** comment every step (“connect signal”, “loop i”, “write line”)

```python
# ❌ BAD
# Close the serial port
self._ser.close()

# ✅ GOOD
# Only one process can own the COM port; leave it free for RDCC streaming.
self._ser.close()
```

### Also prefer

- **Protocol notes** near wire encode/decode (frame shape, sentinel, baud, OSC path)
- **Trap comments** where a wrong default looks fine until runtime (busy serial port, path/port mismatch)
- **Delete or rewrite** comments that no longer match the code; stale “why” is worse than none
- Keep comments sparse: a few high-value ones beat a wall of noise

## Do

- Keep helpers close to use (same module, or the owning package)
- Names carry domain meaning (`pin_slots`, `inactive_sentinel`); lift magic numbers to `settings.py` / `settings.h` when they encode policy
- Leave public APIs stable unless the user asked to reshape them
- Run `compileall` (or firmware verify) after substantive cleanup — see `build.mdc`

## Do not

- Change playback math, stream rates, wire format, defaults, or GUI behavior “while you’re there”
- Rename widely for taste, reshuffle unrelated files, or format the whole project
- Delete debug hooks (widget debug borders, log panel) unless the user asks

## Pass order

1. Resolve scope via git (dirty files, else `HEAD` files); read only those
2. Extract/reuse; simplify conditionals and naming locally
3. Add/fix comments per **Comments**; remove stale narrations
4. Confirm no intentional behavior change; verify if Python/firmware were touched

## Commit

When asked to commit cleanup: `refact:` per `git-commits.mdc`, bullets on structure (extract X, simplify Y), not a file list.
